# ERDOS 902 - THE WHOLE 37-ROW DRT(23,11,5) CATALOGUE, PURE KERNEL.

All 37 published classes of doubly regular tournaments on 23 vertices, decoded
from McKay's `drtourn23.txt` (SHA-256
`20dbad06e128f968e87ce5bec9ca3128b8d2e9a9f25152b29d82c48c967b45d0`,
upper-triangle row-major), and checked BY THE LEAN KERNEL:

* `catalogue_length`    - there are 37 rows here;
* `catalogue_all_drt`   - every one of them is a tournament, 11-out-regular, and
                          every ordered pair of distinct vertices has exactly 5
                          common out-neighbours;
* `catalogue_s3_rows`   - EXACTLY two of the 37 carry S3, namely zero-indexed
                          34 and 35 (the packages' one-indexed rows 35 and 36);
* `row35_is_line34`, `row36_is_line35` - those two rows are literally the ones
                          `F4Rows.lean` counts 2475 and 2530 bad 4-sets in.

Plain `decide` throughout: no `native_decide`, no compiler in the trusted base.

⛔ THE BOUNDARY THAT STAYS A BOUNDARY. This file does NOT prove that these 37
classes are ALL of them. That is the McKay/Spence census (B. D. McKay, and
E. Spence, on doubly regular tournaments / skew-Hadamard matrices of order 24),
a published external result. It is CITED. Nothing here encodes it as proved, and
the downstream seal (`F4Seal.lean`) takes it as an explicit hypothesis instead.
What this file removes from the citation is everything ELSE that used to travel
with it: that the listed objects really are DRTs, that exactly two of them carry
S3, and which two. Those are now kernel facts.
-/

